## ---  3 🧩 React Fragments
Sometimes, you want to return multiple elements from a component, like a heading followed by a list:

tsx
Copy
Edit
function ListGroup() {
  return (
    <h1> HEADING </h1>
    <ul className="list-group">
      <li className="list-group-item"> Item1 </li>
      <li className="list-group-item"> Item2 </li>
    </ul>
  );
}
This will lead to an error because React components must return a single parent element.

You cannot return two sibling elements like <h1> and <ul> side-by-side directly.

Solution: Use React Fragments
React Fragments let you group a list of children without adding extra nodes to the DOM.

You wrap your elements inside a Fragment using the shorthand syntax <>...</>, like this:

function ListGroup() {
  return (
    <>
      <h1> HEADING </h1>
      <ul className="list-group">
        <li className="list-group-item"> Item1 </li>
        <li className="list-group-item"> Item2 </li>
      </ul>
    </>
  );
}

export default ListGroup;
The Fragment <></> acts as a invisible wrapper, allowing you to return multiple elements without extra divs or wrappers cluttering your HTML.

## --- 4 🔄 Dynamic Rendering of List Items
You can render lists dynamically by using JavaScript inside JSX.

For example, given an array of city names:

tsx
Copy
Edit
function ListGroup() {
  const items = ["New York", "London", "Delhi"];

  return (
    <>
      <h1>Cities</h1>
      <ul className="list-group">
        {items.map((item, index) => ( //each list item must have a key property so that react can keep track of the key value pair
          <li key={index} className="list-group-item">{item}</li>   
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  );
}
Explanation:
{items.map(...)} uses JavaScript’s .map() method to iterate over each item.

For each item, it returns an <li> element with the city name.

The key={index} prop is important — React uses keys to identify which items have changed, added, or removed, improving rendering performance.

Note: Using index as key is okay for static lists; for dynamic lists that can reorder or delete items, use a unique stable ID if possible.


## ⚡️ 5 Conditional Rendering

React allows you to render elements conditionally — meaning you can show or hide parts of the UI depending on certain conditions (like the length of a list).

### ✅ Two ways to conditionally render:

```tsx
{items.length === 0 ? <p>No items</p> : null}
```

* This checks if `items.length` is 0.
* If it is, it renders `<p>No items</p>`, otherwise it renders `null` (nothing).

or:

```tsx
{items.length === 0 && <p>No items</p>}
```

* This uses short-circuit evaluation:

  * If `items.length === 0` is `true`, it renders the paragraph.
  * If `false`, it renders nothing.

### Example usage in component:

```tsx
function ListGroup() {
  const items = ["New York", "London", "Delhi"];

  return (
    <>
      <h1>Cities</h1>

      {items.length === 0 && <p>No items</p>}

      <ul className="list-group">
        {items.map((item, index) => (
          <li key={index} className="list-group-item">{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  );
}
```

