## ✅ 9. Passing Functions via Props in React

---

### 🧠 Why Pass Functions via Props?

Sometimes, when an event occurs **inside a child component**, we want the **parent component to react** to it.

For example:

* When an item is clicked in a list, maybe the parent wants to:

  * Show an alert
  * Navigate to another page
  * Update some external state
  * Or just log it

To do this, we **pass a function** from the parent to the child component using **props**.
This is known as **"lifting state up"** or **"function callbacks via props"**.

---

### 📌 Naming Conventions for Functions

There are two common naming conventions used in React:

| Convention | Used in...                    | Example            |
| ---------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------ |
| `handle*`  | Function definition in parent | `handleSelectItem` |
| `on*`      | Prop name in child component  | `onSelectItem`     |

This makes it easier to read and understand:

```tsx
// Parent
const handleClick = () => {}

// Child
<MyComponent onClick={handleClick} />
```

---

## 🧾 Full Working Example

### 🧩 `ListGroup.tsx` — Child Component

```tsx
import { useState } from "react";

interface Props {
  items: string[];
  heading: string;
  onSelectItem: (item: string) => void; // ✅ Function prop
}

function ListGroup({ items, heading, onSelectItem }: Props) {
  const [selectedIndex, setSelectedIndex] = useState(-1);

  return (
    <>
      <h1>{heading}</h1>

      {items.length === 0 && <p>No items</p>}

      <ul className="list-group">
        {items.map((item, index) => (
          <li
            key={index}
            className={
              selectedIndex === index
                ? "list-group-item active"
                : "list-group-item"
            }
            onClick={() => {
              setSelectedIndex(index);        // ✅ Update internal state
              onSelectItem(item);             // ✅ Notify parent
            }}
          >
            {item}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  );
}

export default ListGroup;
```

---

### 🧩 `App.tsx` — Parent Component

```tsx
import Message from "./Message";
import ListGroup from "./components/ListGroup";

function App() {
  let cityItem = ["New York", "London", "Delhi"];

  // ✅ Parent function that handles the selection
  const handleSelectItem = (item: string) => {
    console.log("Selected item:", item);
  };

  return (
    <>
      <div>
        <Message />
        <ListGroup
          items={cityItem}
          heading="Cities"
          onSelectItem={handleSelectItem}
        />
      </div>
    </>
  );
}

export default App;
```

---

## 🔎 Breakdown of Key Concepts

| Concept                   | Explanation                                                                                     |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `onSelectItem`            | A **function passed as a prop** to child component (`ListGroup`).                               |
| `handleSelectItem`        | The function defined in parent (`App.tsx`) which handles what happens when an item is selected. |
| `setSelectedIndex`        | Local state in `ListGroup` to keep track of which item is currently selected.                   |
| `onClick={() => { ... }}` | Anonymous function that updates state and calls the callback.                                   |

